WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.092 --> 00:00:00.925
(phone typing)

00:00:00.925 --> 00:00:02.590
- Watching March Madness reminds me:

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:05.403
Zion Williamson and I
are similarly athletic.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:08.103
What's so funny?

00:00:08.103 --> 00:00:11.160
He's a skinny guy with a funny name,

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.280
and he's the hottest name
in Democratic politics.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.363
Sounds like I'm describing
this guy, right?

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.710
It's actually this guy.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.150
That guy is Pete Buttigieg.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.780
That's B-U-T-T-I-G-I-E-G.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.380
Boom, nailed it; ding!
(bell dinging)

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.340
Boot-edge-edge: That's how
you say it, except fast,

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:34.270
like it says on the
t-shirts his campaign sells.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.110
They really do, look!

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.200
Honestly, just call him "Mayor Pete."

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.530
It's a lot easier, rolls off the tongue.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.430
When Mayor Pete started running
for president in early-2019,

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.840
people, they weren't
calling him much of anything

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.840
because, well, they
had no idea who he was.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.980
Unlike boldfaced names like Beto O'Rourke

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.000
or Bernie Sanders or Joe Biden,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.220
Mayor Pete was considered
a very minor player

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:00.600
in the very crowded 2020 sweepstakes.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.330
After all, he is young, 37 years old,

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.440
relatively inexperienced politically,

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.500
his highest office is his current one,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.740
mayor of South Bend,
Indiana, and has a last name

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.833
that is harder to pronounce than Cillizza.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.330
Cillizza.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.490
Except that politics is
an unpredictable business

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.880
and voters have a tendency
of sometimes surprising you.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.190
The Buttigieg bump,
(bell dinging)

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:26.010
again, began around early-March

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:29.190
when Mayor Pete did a town
hall that was hosted by CNN.

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.140
I know, self-serving, but accurate,

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.450
at the South by Southwest
festival in Austin, Texas.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.920
The mayor got the sort of exposure

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.500
he hadn't been able to
generate to that point,

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.300
and he honestly nailed it.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.250
- I have more years of government
experience under my belt

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:44.688
than the president.

00:01:44.688 --> 00:01:45.892
(audience laughing)

00:01:45.892 --> 00:01:46.860
(audience applauding)

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.515
That's a low bar, I know that.

00:01:48.515 --> 00:01:49.348
(audience laughing)

00:01:49.348 --> 00:01:52.040
I also got more years of
executive government experience

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.540
under my belt than the vice president

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.910
and more military experience than anybody

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:59.400
to walk into that office on
day one since George H.W. Bush.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.580
So, I get that I'm the young
guy in the conversation,

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.300
but I would say experience
is what qualifies me

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.060
to have a seat at this table.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.120
- He was funny, smart, and above all

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.610
spoke and seemed like a normal person.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.810
Suddenly, Buttigieg was everywhere.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.890
- Where are you on the
ideological spectrum?

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:17.960
- I consider myself a
pretty strong progressive,

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.840
but I also don't consider
the left-center spectrum

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.640
to be the most useful way to
look at our politics right now

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.750
because I think it's gotten jumbled up,

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.880
both by the current president
and by the pace of change.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.310
- And after that appearance,
Joe Scarborough said

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:33.030
that there hadn't been so much reaction

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:37.273
to a guest on his show
since, well, this guy.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.650
A clip of Buttigieg speaking Norwegian,

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.760
he's conversant, because of course he is,

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.079
with a reporter from
Norway then went viral.

00:02:48.079 --> 00:02:51.775
- (speaking foreign language)

00:02:51.775 --> 00:02:53.691
- (speaking foreign language)

00:02:53.691 --> 00:02:55.430
- (speaking foreign language)

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:56.990
- Buttigieg's campaign announced

00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:01.170
that he had already secured the
65,000 donors that he needed

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.020
to qualify for the first
Democratic presidential debate

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.610
in June in Miami.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:06.870
He announced in late-March

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:10.680
that he was trying to raise
$500,000 more for his campaign

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.320
in the week or so before the end

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:13.940
of that fundraising quarter.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.150
He raised that amount in 24 hours.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.550
All told, Buttigieg announced
he had raised $7 million

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:22.950
in roughly two months as a candidate,

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.900
a total that, yes, is going to put him

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.150
behind the likes of Sanders and O'Rourke,

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.290
but will put him way in front of where he

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.540
and everyone else thought he would be

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:33.530
at this point in the race.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:37.010
This right now is Mayor Pete's moment.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.260
Every candidate running
for president wants one.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:40.910
Not all get one.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.840
The question going forward
is whether Buttigieg's moment

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.880
is like that time when Herman
Cain led the 2020 GOP field,

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.860
nine-nine-nine, baby,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.600
or more like when that other
skinny guy with the funny name

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.830
started surging in the
2008 Democratic race

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:58.640
and really never stopped.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:03:59.770
There's reason to believe

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:03.440
Buttigieg will last beyond this
current 15 minutes of fame,

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:07.030
and the biggest reason for that
is, well, the mayor himself.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:08.220
- Why are you only showing us

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.470
the positive things about Pete Buttigieg?

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.400
Because that's all we could find,

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:13.233
all right?!
(audience laughing)

00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:14.066
No, I'm being serious.

00:04:14.066 --> 00:04:17.030
There's no dirt on this guy, like nothing.

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:20.620
- Remember that presidential
races, especially in primaries,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.890
are as much about
personality and backstory

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:24.780
as they are about policy.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.920
That's mostly because the candidates

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.070
running for a party's
presidential nomination

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:32.280
tend to agree about the
broad policy solutions

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.690
needed to address things like immigration

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.530
or health care or the economy.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.910
The way that you stand out in a field

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.820
where everyone has very
similar policy plans

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.460
is by the force of your life story

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:46.750
and the way in which you
tell that life story.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:49.550
And Buttigieg, though
he's only 37 years old,

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.370
has a hell of a story to tell.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.650
He's a Harvard grad, a Rhodes scholar,

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.650
he was deployed in Afghanistan
for seven months in 2014,

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.440
and was a Naval Reservist until 2017.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.420
He was elected mayor of
his hometown at age 29.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.150
He is both gay and married.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.890
In short, there's lots of there there

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:10.620
when it comes to Mayor Pete.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.040
His surge has been fueled
less by some catchy slogan

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.760
than by a genuine excitement
among Democratic primary voters

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.950
about his personal story and candidacy.

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:22.860
Plus, the momentum and money

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.930
Buttigieg is gathering right now

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.530
will help him in real tangible ways.

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:29.940
His campaign says it
plans to double his staff

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.130
in the coming weeks

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.630
and they are working on
building bigger organizations

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.580
in places like Iowa and New Hampshire,

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:38.560
where Mayor Pete will have to show well

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.890
to have any chance at
winning the nomination.

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:43.840
Now, before we get too
far ahead of ourselves,

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.470
and I just said, "Any chance
of winning the nomination,"

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.460
it is worth remembering this:
No one, absolutely no one,

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:54.980
will be voting until late-January
or early-February 2020,

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:57.550
which is still a very long time from now.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:00.760
Mayor Pete may be the
flavor of this month,

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.540
but he's unlikely to be the
only lesser-known candidate

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:07.140
who experiences a bit of a
boom between now and next year.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.760
Still, an early Buttigieg bump

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.840
is better than no bump at all.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.240
And lots of Mayor Pete's
fellow 2020 candidates

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.743
would love to have the sort
of run he's been on recently.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:21.570
Campaigns for president are
about creating opportunities,

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:24.350
seizing them, and never letting go.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.660
Pete Buttigieg has already accomplished

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.620
the first two parts of that equation

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.800
in just two months as a candidate.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.720
It's the never-letting-go bit
that he's still got to master.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.860
And that is The Po!nt.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.980
We make new Po!nt episodes
every Tuesday and Thursday.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:41.813
Check 'em out.

